From 84a9c6ff1cc005152ec658c872fc671ed8553c87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peripheral Pretender setup <pp-setup@localhost>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:21:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/57] hw/gpio, hw/timer: peripheral outputs on pads, TCC
 waveform pins

PORT: a pin muxed to a peripheral function is driven by that function's
"pmux-in" line (0, 1, undriven, or floating with the pull off for an
output peripheral that holds the pad); "pmux-active" tells the
peripheral a pad selects it; "pad" gives every driven pad's level; and
each pad's driven-high/driven-low time is accounted on the virtual clock
("pX-stats") so a frontend can show duty cycles.

TCC: waveform outputs WO[x] (channel x modulo CC_NUM, DRVCTRL.INVEN)
drive the pads muxed to them; the counter only stops coasting over
output changes while a pad selects one. Checked on PIC32CM MC00 silicon
through the pad's input buffer: duty CC/(PER+1), floating with the pull
off while disabled, the start-of-cycle level after STOP.

SoC: peripheral pad outputs are wired from the generated signal table
(split-irq fan-out, OR-ed "active" feedback); the generator emits each
TCC's channel count. Boards: the LED from VCC pulls its pad high while
undriven, and drives the LED from "pad".

Fixes: TC and TCC deleted their timer after catching up on a late
callback, stopping event and waveform outputs until the next register
access; PORT re-entered itself when a peripheral drove a pad from its
"pmux-active" callback.

Peripheral Pretender: a personal project; not for upstream.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
